{
  "title": "Anterior Inferior Iliac Spine Avulsion (AIIS)",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/knee-and-sports/3131/anterior-inferior-iliac-spine-avulsion-aiis",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Feb 6 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "An anterior inferior iliac spine (AIIS) avulsion is an apophyseal avulsion injury seen in adolescent athletes as a result of eccentric contraction of the rectus femoris.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made with pelvis radiographs that shows an avulsion off the AIIS.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is nonoperative with rest, icing, NSAIDs and activity modification.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "most often in adolescents between the ages 14-17",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "males more often than females",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "occurs most often in sports involving kicking",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "typically occurs due to **eccentric contraction of the rectus femoris (femoral n.)**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**as hip extends and knee is flexed**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "causes avulsion of its anatomic origin off the pelvis",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Anterior inferior iliac spine",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "a bony prominence just above acetabulum",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3131/images/avulsionillustration.jpg",
                  "caption": "illustration of origins"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "is the origin of the **direct head of the rectus femoris** (femoral n.)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "History",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**sudden \"pop\" in pelvis**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "pain and weakness",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
